  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nBy this weekend, model agreement does start to see the upper-\nlevel ridge begin to break down with the introduction of a new \ntrough pushing into the West Coast. For the Panhandles, these \nsystems will lead to a more zonal to southwesterly flow for the \nweekend that will continue the trend of seeing dry and warm \nconditions. Beyond the weekend however, model agreement is still \ntrending to a full pattern shift as the trough continues to build \nacross the West Coast and force the high pressure over us to move \neast. Should the trend continue then it possible that the \nPanhandles will be seeing a very similar monsoonal set up to what \nwas seen in late September. This would of course lead to decent \namounts of moisture being forced in from both gulfs and chances of\nprecipitation returning as early as Monday. \n\nWhat could be interesting, however, is that models have been\nseeing yet another tropical system getting push up through the\nGulf of California and Central Mexico before being ingest into the\ntrough as it is pushes eastward during the middle of next week.\nThe ingestion of this tropical system would lead to an extra round\nof high moisture over the Panhandles resulting much higher than\nexpected rainfall amounts for both Tuesday and Wednesday. However,\nmodels have been seeing a lot of uncertainty on this tropical\nsystem even coming to be with both the GFS and ECMWF seeing it\ndisappear and reappear with each run. Added to this, the present\nGFS has seen the potential for a dry-slot to push in ahead of the\nactivity Tuesday, which could weaken chances as well. For now,\npresent ensembles have grown more confident in the system bring us\nsome activity with chance of precipitation around 20 to 40 percent\neach day clear into Thursday. Otherwise, look for temperatures to\ncool with passage of this system with afternoon highs lowering in\nthe low to mid 70s.",
